<title>FRANCE: OECD details forecasts of deficit/GDP ratios.</title>
<headline>OECD details forecasts of deficit/GDP ratios.</headline>
<dateline>PARIS 1996-12-19</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development gave the following forecasts for public deficits and debt as a percentage of gross domestic product.</p>
<p>		   (Estimates and projections 1996, 1997 and 1998)</p>
<p>				Public deficit	Gross Public Debt</p>
<p>(*)</p>
<p>			     1996  1997  1998     1996   1997   1998</p>
<p>United States	    -1.6  -1.8  -1.8	 N/A    N/A   N/A</p>
<p>Japan			-4.1  -2.6  -2.3	 N/A    N/A   N/A</p>
<p>Germany **		 -4.1  -3.4  -2.6	61.3   63.2   64.0</p>
<p>France ***		 -4.1  -3.2  -3.0	55.1   56.6   57.7</p>
<p>Italy			-6.7  -3.7  -3.4     124.4  122.9  120.4</p>
<p>United Kingdom	   -4.8  -3.7  -3.0	56.1   56.5   56.3</p>
<p>Canada		     -2.7  -1.5  -0.6	 N/A    N/A    N/A</p>
<p>Total of above countries</p>
<p>			     -3.2  -2.5  -2.2	73.1   73.8   73.7</p>
<p>Australia		  -1.7  -1.1  -0.1	 N/A    N/A    N/A</p>
<p>Austria		    -4.3  -3.0  -3.4	71.8   73.3   74.7</p>
<p>Belgium		    -3.2  -2.9  -2.7     129.9  127.2  124.6</p>
<p>Denmark		    -1.5  -0.4   0.3	71.9   70.4   68.0</p>
<p>Finland		    -2.9  -1.7  -0.7	60.1   60.2   60.2</p>
<p>Greece		     -8.2  -5.7  -5.1     108.5  104.5  100.7</p>
<p>Iceland		    -2.1  -1.0  -0.8	 N/A    N/A    N/A</p>
<p>Ireland		    -1.5  -1.1  -0.5	80.2   76.0   71.0</p>
<p>Netherlands		-2.6  -2.3  -2.1	78.0   76.0   75.7</p>
<p>Norway			5.4   5.0   4.1	 N/A    N/A    N/A</p>
<p>Portugal		   -3.8  -2.9  -2.9	70.3   67.6   66.3</p>
<p>Spain			-4.8  -3.4  -2.9	68.0   68.9   69.2</p>
<p>Sweden		     -3.8  -2.5  -0.5	78.7   78.5   75.8</p>
<p>Total of Above European</p>
<p>  Union Countries	-4.6  -3.3  -2.8	75.0   75.3   75.0</p>
<p>Total of Above</p>
<p>  OECD Countries	 -3.2  -2.5  -2.2	 N/A    N/A    N/A</p>
<p>* The Maastricht definition of gross public debt is based on data provided by the Commission of the European Communities for 1995, projected forward in line with the OECD Secretariat's projections for GDP and general government financial liabilities while taking into account, to the extent possible, of government's policies for acquistion of financial assets. ** Includes balances of the German Railways Fund from 1994 onwards and of the Inherited Debt Fund from 1995 onwards. *** As of 1992, due to several acouting changes, the above deficits calculated using the &quot;Maastricht&quot; definition, are smaller than those published by INSEE, the national statistical agency. NOTE - OECD said projections reflect deficit reduction measures and stated policy intentions where they are embodied in well-defined programmes.</p>
